{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And Mount Sinai smoked in its entirety.</b> The shin of עָשַן has a pataḥ gadol because it is a past-tense verb. Do not wonder on account of the word עָשָן, for he said כְעֶשֶן הַכִּבְשָׁן, and behold he mentioned וַיַּעַל עֲשָׁנוֹ, which is on the pattern of שְׁלָלוֹ זְהָבוֹ, for noun-pattern forms change. And he said שְׁגַר אַלְפֶיךָ (Deuteronomy 7:13) and וְכָל־פֶּטֶר שֶׁגֶר בְּהֵמָה (above, 13:12), and he said וְאֶת־הַבֹּשֶׂם וְאֶת־הַשָּׁמֶן (below, 35:28) and קִנָּמוֹן בֶּשֶׂם (below, 30:23), and furthermore he said אָרִיתִי מוֹרִי עִם־בְּשָׂמִי (Song of Songs 5:1). Now if it were derived from בֹּשֶׂם, it should have had a ḥataf qamatz under the bet—בָּשְׂמִי—like מִקְּדֶשׁ קָדְשִׁי. And if it were derived from וְקִנָּמוֹן בֶּשֶׂם, it should have had a pataḥ under the bet, like מִשֶּׁמֶן שַׁמְנִי וְשַׁמְנִי וְקִטָּרְתִּי (Ezekiel 16:18), or a ḥiriq, like מִצֶּדֶק צִדְקִי. And behold, עִם בְּשָׂמִי, without an added yod, is בָּשָׂם with two qamatzim, like שָׁלָל זָהָב." "<b>And the entire mountain trembled greatly.</b> The opposite of nature, for mountains do not move; only by way of metaphor did he say, וַיָּרֹגְזוּ הֶהָרִים (Isaiah 5:25)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "Resolved the ambiguous citation למעלה י״ג י״ב as Exodus 13:12 based on context."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:36:06.686522311Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}